WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:04.337
Hi, it's our Lady J, and I'm
 here with Karin delaPeña Collison

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:08.133
to talk about her Nicholl
 winning script Coming of Age.

00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:13.680
Wis set in 1965 Britain,
 where a 14-year-old sheltered

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.309
schoolgirl is catapulted
 into the tumultuous backstage

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:23.106
world of the bedroom farce
tour mother stage manages.

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:27.027
Where she experiences
 a scandalous sexual awakening.

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:33.658
Hello!

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.326
Hello.

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.537
I really enjoyed
reading your script.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:38.329
You read it?

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:40.540
I did, of course, 

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.085
thoroughly entertained
 and went on a lovely journey.

00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:46.796
What is the most challenging

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:49.132
part of writing for you?

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:51.426
 When I get a note,
I love getting notes. 

00:00:51.426 --> 00:00:54.137
I did journalism in
one of my incarnations,

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:56.097
and I just thought,
“Oh, this is fantastic.”

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:59.392
People are saying,
 “Edit that out” and I go, “Why?”

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.561
And then I realize,
“Oh, that's so much better.”

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.021
So I've always loved that. 

00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:05.231
I'm glad that you said
 you love getting notes. 

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:08.234
It's one of my favorite things
 as well as long as I have

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.030
a relationship of trust with
the person who's giving the note.

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:13.490
Sometimes,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.575
you do have to push back on a 

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.703
note if your experience
 is very specific.

00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:20.538
I've discovered that 

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:24.959
that my truth is more my boss
 than anybody else.

00:01:25.502 --> 00:01:26.503
Beautifully said. 

00:01:26.503 --> 00:01:29.214
It's also easier
 if you present options

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.049
with saying no, I found.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.425
You know, getting
 a round of notes 

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.803
and going through them all,
 and one standing out is like,

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:37.138
I don't think that works.
 But here's something else.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:39.265
What about this scene? 

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:45.563
So I have heard a lot about
 the specter of the pitching.

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:49.359
It's really just another way
 of performing as an actor.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:51.528
I’m sure you'll be familiar 

00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:53.029
and comfortable with pitching 

00:01:53.029 --> 00:01:55.073
once you get the first
 few out of the way.

00:01:55.406 --> 00:01:57.826
At first, it's very nerve wracking. 

00:01:57.826 --> 00:02:01.287
Can I ask you about
 a eureka moment

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.873
that you found while
 working on this script

00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:06.084
that allowed you
 to break a story?

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.254
I don't think I had any big “Aha”

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.632
you know, except in terms of,
 “Oh, I see how I can solve that.”

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:14.676
“Oh, I see another way I can go there.”

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:18.221
I think one of the reasons
 maybe there wasn't a real eureka

00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:19.556
moment in the script of yours 

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:23.101
is that the characters
 are developed so well.

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:27.105
Often when you spend
 time on characters

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:28.982
and really give them life 

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:32.485
and understand their backstory
 and the choices that

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:36.406
they would make, those choices
 become very easy and very clear.

00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:37.949
When you set them all in a room, 

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:40.368
If you have four
characters you've developed

00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:42.620
and then you bring them all
 in the room together, 

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.415
you're just going to watch them
 talk to each other,

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:46.457
and then you're going to

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:49.127
translate whatever
 they're saying, right?

00:02:49.127 --> 00:02:49.919
It’s true.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.714
What went into creating
 these characters?

00:02:52.714 --> 00:02:56.759
You said part of it was based
on folks that you knew?

00:02:56.759 --> 00:02:58.761
I started in the
family environment 

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:01.472
of my childhood, which
was not an easy childhood.

00:03:01.472 --> 00:03:05.602
But when I started to write,
 I discovered how much joy

00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:07.604
there was there, 

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:10.565
and certainly that wasn't
 anything that I expected.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:15.028
Horowitz, the classical
pianist, once said that

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.446
“Piano shouldn't practice
more than two or three

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:17.280
hours a day.”

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.742
Because “It's living
that creates the artist.”

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:21.951
 and it sounds like you've done

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:26.206
a lot of living in order
 to bring these characters alive.

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:28.833
And it really reads
 and it's really beautiful.

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:29.417
Thank you.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:32.837
I really enjoyed every
moment of reading it

00:03:32.921 --> 00:03:34.380
and chatting with you as well. 

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.506
I have no doubt that you 

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:38.635
will be very successful
 and I look forward to

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:39.719
everything that's to come.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.054
Thank you so much.

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:41.679
Take care.

00:03:42.805 --> 00:03:45.141
She was as lovely
 as I expected her to be.

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:48.311
I'd love to be in her presence
 more and maybe, maybe 

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:51.439
life will present
 that opportunity. We'll see.

